<ArticleLayout title="Apple Watch Stress App Comparison Worksheet: 8 Checks" description="Compare Apple Watch stress apps using eight practical checks: data freshness, missing readings, explanations, subscriptions and privacy. Download the free worksheet." published="2026-09-17" updated="2026-09-17">
<p><strong>Compare stress apps by what you can inspect, not by which one gives you the best-looking score.</strong> Use this eight-check worksheet to record the same day in each app, then choose the presentation that answers your question. This is an editorial evaluation tool from Cortisol+, not a clinical accuracy test or a completed competitor experiment.</p>
<p><a href="/downloads/apple-watch-app-checklist.csv" download>Download the blank comparison worksheet (CSV)</a>. Open it in Numbers, Excel or Google Sheets. There is no signup, and the download does not send your completed answers to us.</p>
<h2>What should I compare?</h2>
<table><caption>Eight checks for an Apple Watch stress app</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Record this</th><th scope="col">Why it helps</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1. Your goal</td><td>Daily stress context, workout recovery or sleep patterns</td><td>A training tool and a stress timeline may answer different questions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Setup</td><td>App version, iPhone/Watch version and permissions you granted</td><td>Features and available inputs can depend on your setup.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Data freshness</td><td>Time of the latest underlying reading and the period being summarized</td><td>An updated screen is not necessarily a newly recorded sample.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Missing data</td><td>Whether absent readings are identified and explained</td><td>Missing data should not be interpreted as a low measurement.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Explanation</td><td>Named inputs, baseline description and what the score means</td><td>A number is more useful when you can understand its basis.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. Daily context</td><td>Whether the view distinguishes exercise, rest and your own observations</td><td>How you feel and a model's interpretation are different records.</td></tr>
<tr><td>7. Access and cost</td><td>Required plan, local price, trial eligibility and renewal period</td><td>Free to download does not establish free access to analysis.</td></tr>
<tr><td>8. Data controls</td><td>Where to review permissions, privacy information and deletion options</td><td>You should understand the choices before sharing health data.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>How do I run a fair comparison?</h2>
<ol><li>Choose one question and one date before opening the apps.</li><li>Use the same device and comparable time windows. Record differences rather than hiding them.</li><li>Mark each check as observed, unclear or unavailable. Do not turn an unknown into a failed feature.</li><li>Keep screenshots privately if they contain personal readings. Record the page or screen that supports each observation.</li><li>Choose by your recorded needs. Do not average scores from different apps or call the highest score the most accurate.</li></ol>
